To create a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text, you can use the `Markdown` library. This library is specifically designed to handle Markdown syntax and convert it to HTML.

Below is a simplified example of how you might implement such a function:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdown.h>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    markdown::Markdown md;
    md.convert(markdown, html);
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello, World!\nThis is a markdown text.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "HTML Output: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

